// Constraint-solver surface for the Plan domain, served on native gRPC.
//
// SolvePlan formulates one plan revision as a constraint scheduling problem (activities into segments,
// candidate assets onto objective resources) and returns candidate schedules together with as much
// explanation as the solve produced: how every solver input was derived from the document, findings
// about inputs that had to be defaulted or excluded, several distinct equally-good schedules with a
// structured diff between them, per-activity reasons when something could not be placed, and a
// minimal conflict set when the whole problem is infeasible.
//
// The endpoint is read-only: nothing is written back to the plan. An objective no schedulable activity
// serves gets one proposed activity for the solve, returned flagged `proposed`; the plan is never written.
//
// A caller writes a schedule back by authoring a `CreatePlanCommit` from one solution and the response
// around it. What differs between schedules is on each solution: the full set of activity placements and
// resource assignments. What every solution shares is on the response, once: `solved_commit`, to use as
// `expected_latest_commit` so a concurrent edit conflicts instead of being clobbered, and
// `proposed_activities`, the new activities that proposed placements refer to.
//
// Solver inputs are read from the plan's first-class fields — `planned_duration`, `constraints`,
// `timezone`, `asset_assignments`, `base_asset_pool` — and, for documents written before those
// fields existed, from the interim YAML block embedded in element descriptions. Both go
// through one adapter, and `problem` reports which source each input came from.
//
// Deliberately its own top-level package rather than `nominal.plan.v1`, and its own HTTP prefix,
// `/plan-solver/v1/`, rather than `/plan/v1/`. combined-service exposes a gRPC service two ways — the
// native gRPC and twirp routes, matched by the fully-qualified `nominal.<package>...<Service>` name,
// and the HTTP routes transcoded from every `google.api.http` annotation — and the ingress allowlist
// matches both by prefix (`/(nominal.asset.*)`, `/(?:api/)?(plan/.*)`, ...). This service's entries
// name it exactly — `/plan-solver/`, the twirp service name and the gRPC service name — rather than
// a package wildcard, so broadening `PlanService`'s entries can never widen the solver's surface and
// widening the solver's can never publish anything under `nominal.plan.v1`. Sharing `PlanService`'s
// package or path would instead tie both surfaces to one entry, so neither could be exposed,
// narrowed or withdrawn on its own. See `.claude/skills/internal-only-services/SKILL.md`; the
// secrets proto migration (#15390) is the precedent.
//
// It still references symbols from `plan.proto` (violations, priorities) — importing a public package
// is fine, it is being *matched* by one that is the hazard.

